rows='''
Campaigns and remaining peoples|Judah and other tribes fight local campaigns. Caleb, Othniel, and Achsah reappear. Many Canaanite populations remain.|The opening records incomplete occupation and forced labor. It qualifies a reading of Joshua as complete removal of earlier inhabitants.|caleb,othniel,achsah,adonibezek|canaan,hebron,debir-town,jerusalem,bethel|The older manual introduces the period’s local leaders. Consider the difference between a narrative summary and each stated result.
The recurring cycle|An angel rebukes Israel. Joshua’s generation dies. Later generations serve other gods, face enemies, and receive deliverers.|The chapter gives the book’s religious explanation for recurring crises. This explanation is distinct from independent historical causes.|joshua|canaan,gilgal|Come, Follow Me studies repentance and the Lord’s repeated willingness to receive people who return.
Othniel, Ehud, and Shamgar|Othniel defeats Cushan-rishathaim. Ehud kills Eglon and leads a revolt against Moab. A short notice records Shamgar’s victory.|The leaders differ in background and tactics. The title judge includes military leadership, not only hearing legal cases.|othniel,cushan-rishathaim,ehud,eglon,shamgar|canaan,moab,jericho,jordan|The Church lesson studies deliverance and repentance. Keep its present application distinct from ancient assassination and warfare.
Deborah, Barak, and Jael|Deborah calls Barak against Sisera. The army defeats Sisera’s force near Kishon. Jael kills the fleeing commander.|Women hold decisive roles in the narrative. The account’s praise does not make its violence a present instruction.|deborah,barak,sisera,jabin-judges,jael,heber|hazor,tabor,kishon|Come, Follow Me studies Deborah’s influence and asks how faith can help other people act.
The song of Deborah|Deborah and Barak sing about the battle, participating tribes, Jael, and Sisera’s mother waiting for his return.|Poetry and the previous prose account present the conflict differently. Proposed dates for the poem remain scholarly interpretations.|deborah,barak,sisera,jael,shamgar|tabor,kishon,megiddo|The older manual studies willing service and courage. Read the poem’s celebration beside its depiction of harm.
Gideon’s call|Gideon receives a call during Midianite raids. He destroys his father’s altar and asks for signs with a fleece.|The account joins a reluctant leader with conflict in his own community. Gideon also receives the name Jerubbaal.|gideon,joash-gideon|ophrah,jezreel|Come, Follow Me studies trust when God’s instructions differ from what a person expects.
Gideon’s reduced force|Gideon’s army is reduced to three hundred. A dream encourages him. Trumpets, jars, and torches precede the enemy’s flight.|The story attributes victory to God rather than numbers. Its troop figures are narrative claims, not measured military records.|gideon,oreb,zeeb|jezreel,jordan|The Church lesson studies reliance on God. Consider how confidence changes when ordinary advantages are removed.
Victory, revenge, and the ephod|Gideon pursues Zebah and Zalmunna, punishes towns that refused help, and kills the kings. He rejects kingship but makes an ephod.|The closing account complicates a simple heroic portrait. The ephod becomes a snare, and Gideon’s household introduces the next conflict.|gideon,zebah,zalmunna,jether,abimelech-judges|succoth,penuel,ophrah|Come, Follow Me studies trust. The older manual also warns about the ephod and acknowledges the account’s change after victory.
Abimelech and Jotham’s warning|Abimelech kills his brothers and becomes king at Shechem. Jotham warns through a tree fable. Civil conflict ends with Abimelech’s death.|The fable questions destructive rule. The account links ambition, family killing, and retaliation against the community.|abimelech-judges,jotham-gideon,gaal,zebul,gideon|shechem,gerizim,thebez|The manual studies Jotham’s warning. Consider what happens when people give authority to a leader who threatens them.
Tola, Jair, and renewed distress|Brief notices record Tola and Jair. Israel serves several gods and faces Philistine and Ammonite pressure. The people confess and remove foreign gods.|The chapter combines short leadership records with the repeated crisis pattern. A stated term of service does not establish calendar dates.|tola,jair|canaan,gilead,ammon|The manual studies the return to God. Consider the actions that make a stated change visible.
Jephthah and his daughter|Gilead’s elders recall the expelled Jephthah to fight Ammon. He makes a vow. His only daughter greets him after victory.|The text says he fulfills his vow but does not narrate the act in detail. Its burnt-offering language produces a tragic reading.|jephthah,jephthah-daughter,moses,sihon,balak|gilead,ammon,heshbon|The older manual favors lifelong dedication over death. Hayes reads the story as sacrifice. The guide preserves this disagreement.
Conflict at the river|Jephthah’s men fight Ephraim and use pronunciation at Jordan crossings to identify fugitives. Short notices record Ibzan, Elon, and Abdon.|Speech becomes a lethal test of group identity. The episode deepens the book’s pattern of violence between related communities.|jephthah,ibzan,elon,abdon|gilead,jordan|The manual compares the conflict with Gideon’s earlier response. Consider how an insult can become collective harm.
Samson’s birth announced|An angel visits Manoah’s wife and later Manoah. The child is to be a Nazirite. Samson is born and begins to receive the Spirit.|The unnamed mother receives the first announcement and reasons calmly after the encounter. A Nazirite is distinct from a person from Nazareth.|samson,manoah,samson-mother|zorah,eshtaol|Come, Follow Me studies covenant faithfulness. The manual treats the angel as God’s messenger rather than naming him as Christ.
The wedding and the riddle|Samson seeks a woman from Timnah. He kills a lion and proposes a riddle. Threats force his wife to obtain its answer.|The wife acts under a threat to burn her family. The episode’s coercion must remain visible rather than becoming a simple betrayal story.|samson,manoah,samson-mother,samson-wife|timnah,ashkelon|The Church lesson studies Samson’s covenant conduct. Read that application beside the threat directed at his wife.
Retaliation at Lehi|Samson’s wife is given to another man. He burns crops. The Philistines burn her household. Samson’s retaliation continues at Lehi.|Harm spreads through reprisals against people, property, and families. Strength does not make each action a moral example.|samson,samson-wife|timnah,lehi|The manual studies Samson’s strength and thirst. It states that linking this Lehi with the Book of Mormon figure lacks evidence.
Delilah and Samson’s final act|Samson visits Gaza. Delilah receives payment to discover his weakness. He is captured and blinded, then destroys the temple during a gathering.|The account joins deception, captivity, prayer, revenge, and many deaths. Delilah’s ethnicity is not explicitly stated in the chapter.|samson,delilah|gaza,sorek|Come, Follow Me studies covenant faithfulness. The older manual distinguishes Samson’s gift of strength from consistent moral conduct.
Micah’s household shrine|Micah returns stolen silver to his mother. Images are made for a shrine. He hires a traveling Levite as priest.|The household seeks religious security through possessions and a hired priest. This Micah is distinct from the later prophetic book’s speaker.|micah-judges,jonathan-priest|ephraim-region,bethlehem|The manual reads this episode as part of religious decline. Consider the difference between a sacred object and faithful conduct.
The Danite migration|Danite scouts consult Micah’s priest. Armed men take the shrine objects and priest, then attack Laish and rename it Dan.|The narrative describes coercion and the destruction of a peaceful town. It does not present the migration as harmless settlement.|micah-judges,jonathan-priest,moses|zorah,eshtaol,ephraim-region,dan-city|The older manual places the migration in the book’s closing decline. Read the inhabitants’ loss beside the migrants’ claim.
The woman at Gibeah|A Levite retrieves his concubine from Bethlehem. At Gibeah, men threaten the guest. The woman is handed over and violently abused.|The woman is unnamed. Her suffering must remain central. Hospitality offered to a man does not excuse exposing a woman to violence.|levite-traveler,levite-concubine|bethlehem,jerusalem,gibeah|The manual treats the closing accounts as moral failure. The woman’s abuse is not an example for readers to follow.
War against Benjamin|The tribes gather at Mizpah after hearing the Levite’s report. Benjamin refuses their demand. Repeated battles bring mass death and destruction.|The Levite’s speech omits his own surrender of the woman. The war turns an atrocity into further collective violence.|levite-traveler,phinehas,eleazar,aaron|mizpah,gibeah,bethel,rimmon-rock|The manual describes the near destruction of Benjamin. Consider how a partial account can influence a community’s judgment.
Women taken and the closing refrain|The assembly regrets Benjamin’s losses. It attacks Jabesh-gilead and arranges the seizure of women at Shiloh.|The attempt to preserve a tribe creates further victims. The closing refrain leaves the book’s disorder unresolved.|benjamin|jabesh-gilead,shiloh,rimmon-rock|The older manual calls the arranged marriages unrighteous. Preserving a group does not excuse kidnapping or forced marriage.
'''.strip().splitlines()
