new=[
('adonibezek','Adoni-bezek','Ruler captured in Judah’s campaign','He recalls his treatment of other kings.','Judges 1:5–7','The account presents his mutilation as a reversal of his own conduct. It does not prescribe that punishment today.'),
('cushan-rishathaim','Cushan-rishathaim','Enemy ruler in Othniel’s account','Named as king of Mesopotamia in the reading text.','Judges 3:8–10','He occupies the oppressor role in the book’s first deliverance cycle. His personal identity is not independently established.'),
('ehud','Ehud','Benjaminite deliverer','Son of Gera. He carries tribute to Eglon.','Judges 3:15–30; 4:1','His left-handed attack and escape drive the account’s defeat of Moabite power.'),
('eglon','Eglon','Moabite king killed by Ehud','He allies with Ammon and Amalek.','Judges 3:12–30','The account portrays him as a tribute-receiving oppressor. This person is distinct from the Canaanite town named Eglon.'),
('shamgar','Shamgar','Briefly recorded deliverer','Son of Anath.','Judges 3:31; 5:6','The book gives a short victory notice and recalls his days in the song. It supplies little biography.'),
('deborah','Deborah','Prophet and judge','The text identifies her as wife of Lappidoth. She calls Barak to lead.','Judges 4–5','She judges disputes, gives direction, and sings about the victory. Her authority is explicit in the account.'),
('barak','Barak','Commander called by Deborah','Son of Abinoam, from Kedesh in Naphtali.','Judges 4–5','He leads the gathered force with Deborah’s support. The song also bears his name.'),
('sisera','Sisera','Commander of Jabin’s army','He flees to Jael’s tent after defeat.','Judges 4–5','His flight ends with Jael’s decisive action. The song contrasts the outcome with his mother’s expectation.'),
('jabin-judges','Jabin','King of Hazor in Deborah’s account','Sisera commands his army.','Judges 4:2,7,23–24','This Jabin belongs to Judges 4. The guide keeps him separate from Joshua’s king of the same name.'),
('jael','Jael','Woman who kills Sisera','Wife of Heber the Kenite.','Judges 4–5','She receives the fleeing commander, then kills him. Both prose and song give her a decisive role.'),
('heber','Heber','Kenite connected with Jael’s household','Husband of Jael. His household has peace with Jabin.','Judges 4:11,17','His treaty relationship explains Sisera’s expectation of safety. The account does not say he directs Jael’s act.'),
('gideon','Gideon / Jerubbaal','Deliverer in the Midianite crisis','Son of Joash and father of Abimelech and Jotham.','Judges 6–9','His account combines reluctance, victory, revenge, rejection of kingship, and an ephod that becomes a snare.'),
('joash-gideon','Joash','Gideon’s father','His altar is destroyed by Gideon.','Judges 6:11,25–32; 7:14; 8:13,29,32','He answers the crowd’s demand to kill Gideon by asking whether Baal can defend himself. This is not the later king Joash.'),
('oreb','Oreb','Midianite commander','Named with Zeeb after the army’s defeat.','Judges 7:25; 8:3','His death appears in the pursuit account. His name also marks the rock named in the narrative.'),
('zeeb','Zeeb','Midianite commander','Named with Oreb after the army’s defeat.','Judges 7:25; 8:3','His death appears in the pursuit account. The guide does not identify his actual appearance or dates.'),
('zebah','Zebah','Midianite king pursued by Gideon','Named with Zalmunna.','Judges 8','The account links his execution with the killing of Gideon’s brothers. Revenge remains part of the story.'),
('zalmunna','Zalmunna','Midianite king pursued by Gideon','Named with Zebah.','Judges 8','He belongs to the pursued royal pair. The narrative gives limited personal detail beyond the conflict.'),
('jether','Jether','Gideon’s firstborn son','Gideon tells him to kill the captive kings.','Judges 8:20','His fear contrasts with Gideon’s command. The text identifies him as young, not as an experienced executioner.'),
('abimelech-judges','Abimelech','Gideon’s son who claims kingship','His mother is from Shechem. Jotham survives his attack on his brothers.','Judges 8:31; 9','His rise and fall show destructive ambition and retaliation. He is distinct from Genesis’s Abimelech.'),
('jotham-gideon','Jotham','Surviving son of Gideon','Brother of Abimelech.','Judges 9','His tree fable warns Shechem about its chosen ruler. He is distinct from the later king Jotham.'),
('gaal','Gaal','Challenger to Abimelech at Shechem','Son of Ebed.','Judges 9:26–41','He speaks against Abimelech and then loses the confrontation. His words help expose the town’s divided loyalties.'),
('zebul','Zebul','Official loyal to Abimelech','He opposes Gaal at Shechem.','Judges 9:28–41','His messages and tactics support Abimelech’s response. He is a person, not the tribal ancestor Zebulun.'),
('tola','Tola','Briefly recorded judge','Son of Puah, of Issachar.','Judges 10:1–2','The account records service and burial with little narrative detail. He is distinct from earlier ancestral names.'),
('jair','Jair','Gileadite judge','The account mentions thirty sons and their towns.','Judges 10:3–5','His household and towns signal local influence. His dates are not independently established.'),
('jephthah','Jephthah','Gileadite commander recalled from exile','Son of Gilead and a woman described as a prostitute. Father of one daughter.','Judges 11–12','He negotiates leadership and argues a territorial case. His vow brings tragedy to his only child.'),
('jephthah-daughter','Jephthah’s daughter','Unnamed only child affected by her father’s vow','Daughter of Jephthah. Her name is not given in Judges.','Judges 11:34–40','She greets her father, requests time with her companions, and becomes the subject of remembrance. Her loss remains central.'),
('ibzan','Ibzan','Briefly recorded judge','The account describes marriages for his sons and daughters.','Judges 12:8–10','His family arrangements mark social connections. The text does not identify him as Boaz.'),
('elon','Elon','Briefly recorded Zebulunite judge','His burial is placed in Aijalon in Zebulun.','Judges 12:11–12','The account gives a short term of service and burial notice. It supplies no extended life story.'),
('abdon','Abdon','Briefly recorded judge','Son of Hillel, from Pirathon.','Judges 12:13–15','His large family and their mounts indicate status in the narrative. They do not establish exact historical dates.'),
('samson','Samson','Nazirite and strongman in the Philistine conflict','Son of Manoah and his unnamed wife.','Judges 13–16','His gifted strength exists beside impulsive conduct and revenge. The account does not make every act a moral example.'),
('manoah','Manoah','Samson’s father','A Danite from Zorah, husband of Samson’s unnamed mother.','Judges 13–16','He seeks instruction after his wife’s angelic encounter. Her explanation answers his fear after the offering.'),
('samson-mother','Samson’s mother','Unnamed woman who receives the birth announcement','Wife of Manoah and mother of Samson.','Judges 13–14','She receives the first message and explains the encounter calmly. The account gives her an active role without naming her.'),
('samson-wife','Samson’s wife at Timnah','Unnamed Philistine woman under threat','Samson’s first wife. Her father later gives her to another man.','Judges 14–15','She reveals the riddle’s answer under threat to burn her household. Later violence kills her and her father.'),
('delilah','Delilah','Woman who discovers Samson’s weakness','Samson loves her. Philistine rulers offer payment for her help.','Judges 16:4–22','Repeated questioning leads to Samson’s capture. The text does not explicitly give her ethnicity or call her a prostitute.'),
('micah-judges','Micah','Owner of a household shrine','His unnamed mother dedicates returned silver. He hires a Levite.','Judges 17–18','He expects security from his shrine and priest but loses them to armed Danites. He is distinct from the prophet Micah.'),
('jonathan-priest','Jonathan','Levite priest taken with Micah’s shrine','Judges 18 identifies him as son of Gershom. The reading text says Moses.','Judges 17–18','The traveling priest accepts a larger tribal position. The final genealogy has a known textual variation, which the guide does not resolve.'),
('levite-traveler','The Levite traveler','Unnamed man whose report helps lead to civil war','Partner of the woman from Bethlehem.','Judges 19–20','He hands the woman to the attackers. His later speech omits that act. The account requires attention to her harm.'),
('levite-concubine','The woman from Bethlehem','Unnamed woman abused at Gibeah','The text calls her the Levite’s concubine.','Judges 19–20','She has almost no recorded speech. The violence against her and use of her body reveal the account’s severe moral failure.')]
